\section{Start with What Is Already Executable}

A normal program loader assumes that code can already run, memory has an established interpretation, and some mechanism supplies the bytes to load. Bootstrap loading supplies those prerequisites in stages. In Levine's account of early PC booting, firmware can start a small fixed-size disk bootstrap, but that bootstrap cannot contain the disk, configuration, executable-format, and memory-management support of a full operating system loader. Each stage can establish a slightly stronger contract for the next \cite[chapter~8]{levine2000linkers}. This is a description of a historical boot path, not a format requirement for x86 machines: for example, UEFI firmware can load a PE/COFF EFI application, not just a legacy boot-sector program.

The important boundary is the handoff. A stage must know where it can place the next image, how the entry point is interpreted, and what stack, processor mode, and input metadata that entry expects. Even a header with a load address, a byte count, and an entry offset requires checking all three: a short read cannot establish the claimed initialized extent, and an entry offset outside the loaded code cannot begin the next stage. Passing a pointer to a memory map is also an ownership decision. If the next stage reuses its storage before reading it, the pointer is worthless even though its numerical value has not changed.

There is a second, less literal bootstrap in a dynamically linked GNU/Linux process. The kernel already exists; it can map the program and the ELF interpreter designated by \texttt{PT\_INTERP}. The interpreter runs first, completes the required relocation and dependency work, and transfers toward the program's entry. Its earliest code cannot rely on symbolic references that only its own relocation will make usable \cite[chapter~10]{levine2000linkers}. The interpreter bootstraps the user's process, not the machine.

\section{Overlays Give the Same Address Several Meanings}

In the tree-structured overlay systems Levine describes, used on mainframes such as OS/360 and later by some DOS linkers, a program can be larger than the program memory available to it. Its root stays resident; different descendants can occupy the same address range at different times. A linker places code using its eventual memory address but gives each overlay its own location in the executable file. It records a descriptor with the file location, memory destination, length, and parent, and redirects downward calls through glue code to ensure that the callee's path is resident.

This differs from demand paging. Two virtually distinct pages can share a physical frame at different times without changing the meaning of either virtual address. If overlays A and B use the same program address, by contrast, a raw pointer to that address cannot distinguish the two. A root-level descriptor identifying an overlay and an entry offset can make that distinction; a function pointer into an evictable overlay cannot safely be saved and invoked without ensuring the correct overlay is loaded. Mutable state that must survive an eviction belongs in resident storage, or must be explicitly saved and restored.

