\section{Switch Branches Only When the Stack Permits It}

Consider a single-threaded teaching machine with a \(1024\)-byte code budget, disjoint from stack and resident data. The root occupies bytes \([0,256)\). Its children A and B start at \(256\) and occupy \(384\) and \(512\) bytes respectively. A's child C occupies \(256\) bytes starting at \(640\). With these placements the deepest path ends at \(896\), whereas separate storage for all four code bodies would take \(1408\) bytes. The linker has already relocated each body for its assigned address. The resident manager makes the switch; loading never overwrites its own text.

\begin{verbatim}
call_overlay(target):
    if target.overlay in resident_path:
        jump target.load_address + target.entry_offset
    wanted = path(root, target.overlay)
    prefix = common_prefix(resident_path, wanted)
    victims = resident_path after prefix
    # Include the return address of the current call.
    require no live return PC belongs to any victim
    forget victims                  # do not claim old bytes on error
    for node in wanted after prefix:
        copy_exactly(node.file_extent, node.load_address)
        synchronize_instruction_fetch_if_needed(node)
        append node to resident_path  # only after a successful copy
    jump target.load_address + target.entry_offset
\end{verbatim}

From the root, a call to C at entry offset \(16\) loads A and then C, leaving execution to begin at address \(656\). Once that synchronous call returns to the root, a call from the root to B can discard A and C. B's bytes cover \([256,768)\), overwriting all of A and part of C. Even if some bytes of C happen to survive, none of C remains callable as a resident overlay.

