For a failure test, give C a normal returning call to B. It would replace code containing a live return address in C. A tree overlay linker should reject that cross-branch call; the resident-path check illustrates why. An upward call is different: the ancestor is already resident. But if it then tries to load another branch, it must still account for the suspended descendant's return address. The invariant is that each live return lands in the same code that was present when the call was made.

The same capacity calculation yields different costs depending on execution order. Calling C twice from the root can reuse A and C after the first load. Calling C, then B, then C must load \(640+512+640=1792\) bytes, even though only \(896\) bytes are needed to accommodate any one path. This is why the overlay tree reflects the program's call patterns and not just its module sizes. Like the PLT, its glue mediates a transfer without changing the calling convention; unlike a PLT slot that remains bound, an overlay's residency can disappear after a different call.
